# workoutLab web app — dev environment (T-0902, AC1).
#
# Copy this file to `apps/web/.env.local` (gitignored) and fill in both values.
# Vite only auto-loads `.env*` from this directory (`apps/web/`), NOT from the repo root,
# so the root `.env.local` (which holds the Terraform/Supabase CLI secrets) is deliberately
# not read here. These two are the only vars the browser bundle needs.
#
# Both are safe to expose to the browser: the anon key is a public, RLS-scoped key. Never put
# the service-role key in a VITE_* var — anything VITE_-prefixed is inlined into the bundle.
#
# Where to get them:
#   - Hosted project: Supabase dashboard > Project Settings > API
#       Project URL -> VITE_SUPABASE_URL
#       Project API keys > anon/public -> VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
#   - Local stack (`npx supabase start`): run `npx supabase status -o env` and use
#       API_URL  -> VITE_SUPABASE_URL   (normally http://127.0.0.1:54321)
#       ANON_KEY -> VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
#     Magic-link emails are caught by Mailpit at http://127.0.0.1:54324
#
# If either is missing, `pnpm --filter @workoutlab/web dev` still starts and the shell paints:
# auth is disabled, the app stays signed out, and the console carries one warning naming both
# vars (AC2, AC-A5). A production `build` still fails loudly, because the CSP `connect-src` is
# derived from the URL (AC5, AC-A10).

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
